Below is a practical, industry-standard checklist of what a User Management system is expected to provide today, followed by implementation guidance in plain Java (no frameworks).

I’ll separate “must-have” vs “enterprise-grade” so you can scope it realistically.

1. Core (Industry-Standard) User Management Features

These are expected in almost every professional system.

1.1 User Identity

Must have

Unique user ID (UUID preferred)

Username or email (unique)

Password (hashed, never stored in plain text)

Account status:

ACTIVE

DISABLED

LOCKED

PENDING_VERIFICATION

Recommended

Display name

Created date / last updated

Last login timestamp

1.2 Authentication

Must have

Login with username/email + password

Secure password storage:

BCrypt / PBKDF2 / Argon2

Password verification

Logout / session invalidation

Recommended

Account lock after N failed attempts

Password expiration policy (optional)

1.3 Authorization (Roles & Permissions)

Must have

Role-based access control (RBAC)

User ↔ Roles (many-to-many)

Role ↔ Permissions (many-to-many)

Example

Roles: ADMIN, USER, SUPPORT

Permissions: USER_READ, USER_CREATE, USER_DELETE

Industry expectation

Authorization checked at service/business layer

Not hard-coded if/else logic

1.4 Password Management

Must have

Change password

Password reset (token-based)

Password strength validation

Industry standard

Reset tokens:

Random

Single-use

Expiry (e.g., 15–30 minutes)

1.5 Account Lifecycle

Must have

Create user

Update user

Disable / enable user

Soft delete (do NOT hard delete by default)

Recommended

Email verification on signup

Reactivation flow

2. Security Requirements (Non-Negotiable)

These are industry expectations, not optional.

2.1 Password Security

Hash + salt

Use adaptive hashing (BCrypt / PBKDF2)

Never log passwords

Never return passwords in responses

2.2 Session Management

If using sessions

Secure session ID

Session timeout

Session invalidation on logout

Regenerate session ID on login

If using tokens

Signed tokens

Expiration

Revocation strategy

2.3 Brute Force Protection

Failed login attempt counter

Temporary account lock

Optional IP throttling

2.4 Auditing & Logging

Must have

Login success/failure logs

Password change logs

Role/permission changes

