4.2 Password Hashing Example (Java Only)
public class PasswordHasher {

    public static String hash(char[] password, byte[] salt) {
        try {
            PBEKeySpec spec = new PBEKeySpec(password, salt, 65536, 256);
            SecretKeyFactory skf =
                SecretKeyFactory.getInstance("PBKDF2WithHmacSHA256");
            byte[] hash = skf.generateSecret(spec).getEncoded();
            return Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(hash);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Hashing error", e);
        }
    }
}

4.3 Authentication Flow (Industry Standard)
Login Request
→ Validate input
→ Load user
→ Check status
→ Verify password
→ Check failed attempts
→ Create session / token
→ Update last_login
→ Audit log

4.4 Authorization Check Example
if (!authorizationService.hasPermission(user, "USER_DELETE")) {
    throw new AccessDeniedException();
}

5. Advanced / Enterprise Features (Optional)

Only add these when needed:

Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)

OAuth2 / SSO

Device tracking

IP whitelisting

Fine-grained permissions (attribute-based access control)

GDPR data export & anonymization

6. Common Mistakes to Avoid

❌ Storing plain passwords
❌ Hard-coded role checks
❌ No account lockout
❌ No audit logging
❌ Mixing authentication & business logic

7. Minimum Viable “Industry-Ready” Checklist

If you want a resume-worthy system, make sure you have:

✔ Secure password hashing
✔ RBAC (roles + permissions)
✔ Password reset tokens
✔ Account lock on failed login
✔ Audit logs
✔ Clean separation of layers